@kind(writ)

#[ Writ: tomet-syntax-parser ]

Rules this crate must obey.

Scope is this directory. Rules that bind more than this crate live in a
`.writ.tmt` further up; read every `.writ.tmt` from here to the workspace
root, the way `.gitignore` is read.

Only the author edits this file. An agent that finds a rule in its way
proposes an edit here and stops. It does not work around the rule, and it
does not relax the rule to fit the change it wanted to make.

##[ parser-purity ]

The parser is pure, deterministic and side-effect-free. It performs no
I/O, resolves no external file, and executes nothing. The same input text
produces the same tree, in predictable time.

File resolution is `tomet-semantics-resolver`'s job -- Tomet's
preprocessor/linker layer, and the only place reading a referenced file is
allowed. `@config(import:)` and `@settings(file:)` are resolved there,
after parsing.

---[ Why ]---

Every consumer holds a `Document` and needs to trust it. A parser that
reads the filesystem gives a different tree depending on what is on disk,
which turns every downstream test into an integration test and makes an
LSP buffer disagree with the file it came from.

It is also what lets the tree be cached and compared at all. The
vocabulary guard below works by parsing two sources and comparing trees;
that comparison means nothing if parsing can consult anything but its
input.

---[ Guard ]---

None yet. `tomet-syntax-parser`'s `[dependencies]` carry nothing that can
do I/O today, which is the property `twrit` should pin: no `std::fs`, no
`std::net`, and no dependency that provides them.

##[ no-vocabulary ]

The parser builds the tree without consulting any element vocabulary.
Renaming an element -- or renaming a key inside its `(args)` -- changes the
name recorded in the tree and nothing else about its *shape*. External files
may add constraints or change how a document renders; they must never change
the tree.

This says nothing about what the tree *means*. Classification, validation
and evaluation read the vocabulary; that is their job and it happens after
parsing.

---[ Why ]---

Built-ins and user-defined elements stay symmetric. The moment the parser
recognizes `codeblock`, no element a user writes can behave like
`codeblock`, and the format has started growing by privileging its own
names. `fact/root.tmt` promises the opposite: identifier, input, data, and a
setting that says where each one goes.

It also keeps the grammar still. If element names reached into parsing, then
adding an element would be a grammar change -- `tree-sitter-tomet`'s
hand-maintained approximation and every downstream consumer would have to
move with it.

The rejected alternative is worth writing down, because it is the one that
keeps looking cheaper: special-case the element in the parser. That is what
this parser used to do, in `is_codeblock`, `is_verbatim_content`,
`local_format_key`, `is_format_target_element` and `is_config`. Each branch
was locally reasonable. The accumulated cost was a `running_format` mode
threaded through the parser, and content that lexed differently depending on
the element name above it. Meaning migrated from settings into the grammar
one branch at a time, and nothing pointed at the accumulation.

---[ Guard ]---

@link(file:../../tests/src/vocabulary.rs)[`tests/src/vocabulary.rs`]

Each case is a pair of sources differing in exactly one identifier the
parser is not allowed to recognize. Both are parsed, the identifier is
normalized to a shared placeholder, and the two trees are compared. `Span`
compares equal unconditionally, so the comparison is on shape. Every case
names the branch it pins down -- a branch this parser used to have.

Known gap: the guard has no case asserting that it still detects a
violation. Gut the comparison and it stays green forever. A reintroduced
vocabulary lookup would be caught today; a quietly broken guard would not.
